latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Extract the color sequence:\n   a. Iterate through all cells in the input grid.\n   b. Collect unique colors that are not black (0) or gray (5).\n   c. Maintain the order of colors as they appear in the grid.\n\n2. Determine the fill direction:\n   a. Calculate the average column of the color cluster.\n   b. If the average is less than half the grid width, set direction to left-to-right; otherwise, right-to-left.\n\n3. Create a color cycle:\n   a. Use the extracted color sequence to create an infinite cycle of colors.\n\n4. Define a function to get the color for any coordinate in an infinite diagonal pattern:\n   a. Take row and column as input.\n   b. Calculate the diagonal index based on the fill direction.\n   c. Use the diagonal index to get the appropriate color from the color cycle.\n\n5. Process the input grid:\n   a. Create a new grid with the same dimensions as the input.\n   b. Iterate through each cell in the input grid:\n      - If the cell is gray (5):\n        * Calculate its color using the function from step 4.\n        * Place this color in the corresponding position in the new grid.\n      - If the cell is not gray:\n        * Copy the original color to the new grid.\n\n6. Return the new grid as the result.\n\nDetailed implementation of key steps:\n\nStep 1 (Extract color sequence):\n```python\ncolor_sequence = []\nfor row in input_grid.values:\n    for cell in row:\n        if cell not in [0, 5] and cell not in color_sequence:\n            color_sequence.append(cell)\n```\n\nStep 2 (Determine fill direction):\n```python\nrows, cols = input_grid.get_dimensions()\ncolor_positions = [(r, c) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols) if input_grid.values[r][c] in color_sequence]\navg_col = sum(c for _, c in color_positions) / len(color_positions)\nfill_direction = 1 if avg_col < cols / 2 else -1\n```\n\nStep 3 (Create color cycle):\n```python\nfrom itertools import cycle\ncolor_cycle = cycle(color_sequence)\n```\n\nStep 4 (Function to get color for any coordinate):\n```python\ndef get_color(row, col):\n    diagonal_index = row + (col if fill_direction == 1 else -col)\n    return next(color_cycle) for _ in range(diagonal_index % len(color_sequence)))\n```\n\nStep 5 (Process the input grid):\n```python\nnew_grid = []\nfor r, row in enumerate(input_grid.values):\n    new_row = []\n    for c, cell in enumerate(row):\n        if cell == 5:  # gray\n            new_row.append(get_color(r, c))\n        else:\n            new_row.append(cell)\n    new_grid.append(new_row)\n```\n\nThis plan should work for all cases, maintaining the diagonal pattern across disconnected gray areas and respecting the fill direction based on the color cluster position."
